{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-09-15T03:05:25.537Z","sessionId":"9c1fe48d-8404-456e-b65e-61c070870312","content":"Review the conversation context below and respond with a concise summary\nof important items that should be preserved in the daily log.\nDo NOT use any tools — just return plain text.\n\nFormat your response as a structured daily log entry with these sections:\n\n**Context:** [One line about what the user was working on]\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- [Important Q&A or discussions]\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- [Any decisions with rationale]\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- [Gotchas, patterns, or insights discovered]\n\n**Action Items:**\n- [Follow-ups or TODOs mentioned]\n\nSkip anything that is:\n- Routine tool calls or file reads\n- Content that's trivial or obvious\n- Trivial back-and-forth or clarification exchanges\n\nOnly include sections that have actual content. If nothing is worth saving,\nrespond with exactly: FLUSH_OK\n\n## Conversation Context\n\n**Assistant:** dcg blocked the shell redirect. Using the Write tool instead.\n\n**Assistant:** Now updating the index line so the hook matches the wider trap.\n\n**Assistant:** Commit landed; remote moved under me. Merging and retrying.\n\n**Assistant:** Saved — will be active from next session.\n\n**Done:**\n- `project_settings-json-bom-trap.md` rewritten. It now covers both faults: the BOM **and** the mangled characters, plus the fact that `CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.md` get hit too, not just `settings.json`.\n- Index line in `MEMORY.md` updated so the hook mentions the junk statusline bars.\n- Committed and pushed.\n\n**One thing you should look at**\n\nWhile pushing, a new commit came down from your Windows laptop:\n\n```\ncd8e9bb  RECOVERY-INVENTORY: the Windows laptop has no working backup\n```\n\nIt added `~/.claude/RECOVERY-INVENTORY.md` and 5 new PowerShell survey scripts. That is not something I did, and the title sounds important.\n\nWant me to read it and tell you what it says?\n\n**User:** yes, read it\n\n**Assistant:** Read it. Here is the short version.\n\n## What it says\n\n**Your Windows laptop has no working backup.** Anything pushed to GitHub is safe. Everything else on that machine exists in **one place only**.\n\n**Safe:** all 131 repos, `~/.claude`, skills, global memory, Google Drive documents.\n\n**Not safe — about 15 GB, single copy:**\n\n| Thing | Size |\n|---|---|\n| `SportScienceResearchRepo` working data | 4.37 GB |\n| `Football` folder | 2.94 GB |\n| `Risk_Stratification_Engine` | 1.80 GB |\n| `ForcePlate_DecisionSystem` (with the database) | 1.69 GB |\n| `~/.codex` session history | large |\n\n**Why it broke — three reasons:**\n\n1. All nine KeystoneOS scheduled tasks on Windows are **Disabled**. Switched off during the Mac move, never switched back on.\n2. Two replacement tasks were written, but **registered with no trigger**. They say `Ready`. They will never fire. No last run time, no next run time.\n3. The backup manifest on Windows is the **Mac's manifest**. I checked it — every path is a Mac path (`/Volumes/CRASHBOX`, `/Users/kristinarash/...`). Only the GitHub line works on Windows. Last backup state written there: **2026-08-14, status ERROR**. Nearly a month stale.\n\n## One thing in the report is wrong\n\nIt says the two backup scripts sit in `DEV\\_INBOX\\` and are \"not in any repo either\" because `dev-sync` skips folders starting with `_`.\n\n**That is not true.** I checked:\n- `dev-sync` only skips `.claude`, `_tools`, `_DRIVE`, `token-dashboard`, and `*_OLD_merged*`. `_INBOX` is not on that list.\n- `_INBOX` is its own repo at `github.com/erash11/_INBOX`, clean and pushed.\n- Both scripts are on this Mac right now at `~/DEV/_INBOX/backup_dev.ps1` and `backup_ai_sessions.ps1`.\n\nSo the scripts **are** backed up. Fix #2 in that report is unnecessary. The other findings still stand.\n\n## The real fix\n\nThe report ranks it right. Item 1 is almost all the value:\n\n**Give the two Windows tasks a trigger.** They are already written and registered. One `schtasks` change each. That is the whole difference between 15 GB single-copy and 15 GB backed up.\n\nOne warning worth keeping: `backup_dev.ps1` uses `/MIR`, which mirrors. If you delete something by accident, a mirror copies the deletion into your backup. Change it to `/E`.\n\n## Your call\n\nI cannot run `schtasks` from here — it must happen on the Windows laptop.\n\n- **A)** I fix the scripts now on the Mac (drop `/MIR`, add a Windows manifest), push them, and write you the exact `schtasks` commands to paste on Windows.\n- **B)** Leave it until you are next on Windows.\n\nI'd go with A. The edits are safe from here, and it means your Windows session is one paste instead of a debugging job.\n\n**User:** A\n\n**Assistant:** Both scripts read. `backup_ai_sessions.ps1` is already additive (`/E`) — good. Only `backup_dev.ps1` uses `/MIR`. Let me see the manifest structure.\n\n**Assistant:** There is already a `daily-backup.ps1` for Windows that reads the manifest. Let me check it before I add anything.\n\n**Assistant:** The picture changed. Let me think this through before editing.\n\n**Assistant:** Good — that confirms the plan and adds two traps I'd have missed. Applying now.\n\n**Assistant:** Now the Windows manifest overlay.\n\n**Assistant:** Both edits are correct. Now writing the paste-ready Windows commands, then committing.\n\n**Assistant:** Done. Let me verify what landed.\n\n**Assistant:** All done and pushed. Here is what you have.\n\n## What I changed\n\n**1. `~/DEV/_INBOX/backup_dev.ps1`** — changed `/MIR` to `/E`.\n\nNow it adds files and never deletes. Before, if you deleted something by accident, the next backup would copy that deletion into your backup too.\n\n**2. `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.windows.json`** — new file.\n\nRecords the real Windows backup destinations. Before, Windows was reading the Mac's list, where only the GitHub line works.\n\nBeing honest: **nothing reads this file yet.** It is documentation until someone builds a Windows checker. I wrote that inside the file so it does not look more finished than it is.\n\n**3. `~/DEV/_INBOX/FIX-WINDOWS-BACKUP.md`** — new file. This is the main thing.\n\nPaste-ready commands for the Windows laptop, in order.\n\n## What you do on Windows\n\nRun `/dev-sync` there first, then open `DEV\\_INBOX\\FIX-WINDOWS-BACKUP.md` and work down it.\n\nThree things in it you would have hit otherwise:\n\n- **`schtasks /Change` will not work.** It cannot add a schedule to a task that has none. The file uses `Set-ScheduledTask` instead.\n- **`G:` only exists when you are logged in.** It is a per-user Google Drive mount. If the task runs as SYSTEM, it sees no `G:` and writes nothing, silently. The file checks for this.\n- **A 2 AM trigger on a sleeping laptop never fires.** So each task gets two triggers: a set time *and* at-logon, plus `-StartWhenAvailable` so a missed slot catches up.\n\nThe file ends by making you check that **bytes actually landed in Google Drive** — not just that the task exists. That is the check the whole report says was missing everywhere.\n\n## One thing I found that the report got wrong\n\nI said earlier its fix #2 was unnecessary. Now I know why it matters that you skip it: the scheduled task was registered pointing at `DEV\\_INBOX\\`. **Moving the scripts would break the task.** Leave them where they are.\n\n## A warning I added\n\n**Do not re-enable the nine KeystoneOS tasks**, including `KeystoneOS-DailyBackup`.\n\n`~/.claude/scripts/daily-backup.ps1` is no longer the Windows script. It was replaced by the Mac version on 2026-08-05 — it uses `rsync` and Mac paths. Turning those tasks back on would run the Mac script on Windows and fail. The old Windows one is frozen at `daily-backup.ps1.win-orig`.\n\n## Heads up\n\nYour Windows laptop is pushing commits **right now**, while I was working. Three separate pushes landed and I had to merge each time:\n\n- `RECOVERY-INVENTORY` (22:30)\n- `survey-*` scripts\n- `verify-crossmachine.ps1`, `review-prep.ps1` (just now)\n\nSomething is actively running over there. If that is you, fine. If not, worth a look.\n\n**User:** <task-notification>\n<task-id>bpnwwmrvm</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01SeLaSFZrfeyg1pAAsaRTiQ</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/7741462f-dac7-4551-8692-76574bbfd202/tasks/bpnwwmrvm.output</output-file>\n<status>killed</status>\n<summary>Background command \"Find local folders for the five repos flagged missing\" was stopped because the system is running low on memory</summary>\n</task-notification>\n\n**Assistant:** That was the slow folder search I started earlier and replaced with a faster one. It got killed for using too much memory.\n\n**No action needed.** I already answered that question another way — all five repos were accounted for (`AI_Analyst` in `_ARCHIVE`, `Linda Projects`, `Website`, `claude-global-memory`, and `Injury_Surveillance` which I cloned).\n\nNothing is lost. Your sync and the Windows backup fix are both finished and pushed.\n\n**User:** do i need to do anything with this?\n\n**Assistant:** No. That one is closed — nothing for you to do.\n\nLet me check one thing that did change while we worked.\n\n**Assistant:** **No. Nothing to do with the killed task.** That is closed.\n\nBut something else landed on your Mac while we worked, and you should know about it:\n\n## Your skills got cut from ~160 to 63\n\nA commit called **\"apply global skill audit\"** came in at **12:12 today**, authored by you. It deleted **913 files**. It is already applied here and pushed. Your Mac and GitHub agree.\n\nIt also wrote a new memory note: `project_skill-bloat-and-promotion.md` — \"the 2026-09-12 skill audit (160→61), the project-until-promoted rule\".\n\nSo this looks **deliberate**, done by you from the other machine.\n\n## Two things to check\n\n**1. Was this you?** If you ran a skill audit today, you are fine — ignore the rest.\n\nIf you did **not** do this, tell me and I will look. Nothing is lost either way — `git` keeps every deleted skill, and I can bring any of them back.\n\n**2. `/backup` is still not run.** From this morning's list, and still true. Your CRASHBOX SSD backup has not happened.\n\n## Everything from this session is finished\n\n- DEV sync: clean, 136 repos, 1 cloned\n- Statusline: fixed\n- `CLAUDE.md` + `AGENTS.md`: encoding repaired\n- Memory note: updated\n- Windows backup fix: written and pushed to `DEV\\_INBOX\\FIX-WINDOWS-BACKUP.md`\n\nThe only open item you own is running that Windows file when you are next on that laptop.\n\n**User:** what windows file? what do I need to run?\n\n**Assistant:** My wording was bad. It is **not a file you run.** It is a page of instructions with commands to copy.\n\n## The problem it fixes\n\nYour Windows laptop has **no working backup**. About 15 GB there exists in one place only. If that laptop died today, it is gone.\n\nThe cause: two backup tasks exist on that laptop, but nobody gave them a **start time**. They sit there saying \"Ready\" forever and never run.\n\n## What you do\n\nNext time you are on the Windows laptop:\n\n**1.** Run `/dev-sync` there. That pulls down what I wrote today.\n\n**2.** Open this file and follow it:\n\n```\nC:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\_INBOX\\FIX-WINDOWS-BACKUP.md\n```\n\nIt is written as copy-paste blocks, in order.\n\n## The core of it\n\nIf you just want the main fix, this is it. Open **PowerShell 7 as admin**, logged in as you, and paste:\n\n```powershell\n$devTriggers = @(\n  (New-ScheduledTaskTrigger -Weekly -DaysOfWeek Sunday -At 6pm),\n  (New-ScheduledTaskTrigger -AtLogOn -User \"$env:USERDOMAIN\\$env:USERNAME\")\n)\nSet-ScheduledTask -TaskName \"Backup DEV to Drive\" `\n  -Trigger $devTriggers `\n  -Settings (New-ScheduledTaskSettingsSet -StartWhenAvailable -ExecutionTimeLimit (New-TimeSpan -Hours 4))\n\n$aiTriggers = @(\n  (New-ScheduledTaskTrigger -Daily -At 5:30pm),\n  (New-ScheduledTaskTrigger -AtLogOn -User \"$env:USERDOMAIN\\$env:USERNAME\")\n)\nSet-ScheduledTask -TaskName \"Backup AI Sessions to Drive\" `\n  -Trigger $aiTriggers `\n  -Settings (New-ScheduledTaskSettingsSet -StartWhenAvailable -ExecutionTimeLimit (New-TimeSpan -Hours 1))\n```\n\nThen check it worked:\n\n```powershell\nGet-ScheduledTask | Where-Object TaskName -like \"*Backup*\" |\n  Get-ScheduledTaskInfo | Select-Object TaskName, NextRunTime\n```\n\n**`NextRunTime` must show a date.** If it is blank, it still will not run.\n\n## Why the file has more than that\n\nThree things can still silently break it, and the file walks you through each:\n\n- The task might be running as SYSTEM, which cannot see your `G:` drive\n- The task names might be different from what I guessed\n- The task might exist and fire but copy nothing\n\nThe last section makes you check that **files actually appeared in Google Drive** — not just that the task looks healthy. That is the whole lesson from your own recovery report."}
