{"parentUuid":"eb8f2a1a-bc88-450b-8378-1e906b02aa78","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"6b83a2f2-5d3e-4731-b48d-723b14b644b6","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\":{\"hookEventName\":\"SessionStart\",\"additionalContext\":\"\\n<context_window_protection>\\n  <priority_instructions>\\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\\n  </priority_instructions>\\n\\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \\\"timeline\\\")\\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\\n       - Each command: {label: \\\"section header\\\", command: \\\"shell command\\\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\\\"q1\\\", \\\"q2\\\", ...])\\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n\\n  <when_not_to_use>\\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\\n  </when_not_to_use>\\n\\n  <file_writing_policy>\\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\\n  </file_writing_policy>\\n\\n  <output_constraints>\\n    <artifact_policy>\\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\\n    </artifact_policy>\\n  </output_constraints>\\n  <session_continuity>\\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\\n  </session_continuity>\\n\\n  <ctx_commands>\\n    \\\"ctx stats\\\" | \\\"ctx-stats\\\" | \\\"/ctx-stats\\\" | context savings question\\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx doctor\\\" | \\\"ctx-doctor\\\" | \\\"/ctx-doctor\\\" | diagnose context-mode\\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx upgrade\\\" | \\\"ctx-upgrade\\\" | \\\"/ctx-upgrade\\\" | update context-mode\\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx purge\\\" | \\\"ctx-purge\\\" | \\\"/ctx-purge\\\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\\n\\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \\\"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\\\"\\n  </ctx_commands>\\n\\n</context_window_protection>\"}}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/sessionstart.mjs\"","durationMs":721},"type":"attachment","uuid":"2b458323-eff3-46c1-9434-125145f00cf0","timestamp":"2026-09-15T03:05:25.335Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"9c1fe48d-8404-456e-b65e-61c070870312","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"2b458323-eff3-46c1-9434-125145f00cf0","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"6b83a2f2-5d3e-4731-b48d-723b14b644b6","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"/last30days: Ready to use. Run /last30days to get started — setup takes 30 seconds.\n  Research any topic across Reddit, HN, X, YouTube, Polymarket (last 30 days).\n\nReddit, Hacker News, Polymarket, and YouTube (yt-dlp detected) work out of the box.\nThe setup wizard can unlock X/Twitter and more.\n  Detected: yt-dlp is installed (YouTube transcripts ready, no setup needed).","stdout":"/last30days: Ready to use. Run /last30days to get started — setup takes 30 seconds.\n  Research any topic across Reddit, HN, X, YouTube, Polymarket (last 30 days).\n\nReddit, Hacker News, Polymarket, and YouTube (yt-dlp detected) work out of the box.\nThe setup wizard can unlock X/Twitter and more.\n  Detected: yt-dlp is installed (YouTube transcripts ready, no setup needed).\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"bash \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/scripts/check-config.sh\"","durationMs":731},"type":"attachment","uuid":"f40409d2-babc-437b-a64b-e272b94c22e7","timestamp":"2026-09-15T03:05:25.345Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"9c1fe48d-8404-456e-b65e-61c070870312","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"f40409d2-babc-437b-a64b-e272b94c22e7","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_additional_context","content":["## User profile (global-memory/USER.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 1,375 chars. Updated when the agent learns durable user preferences. -->\n# User Profile\n\n## About\nEric Rash — Director of Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Builds athlete performance data pipelines (Football GPS, force plate). Family: wife Kristina; kids Ames, Saylor, Rowan (5, 4, 10mo as of 2026-09-01). Career target: analytics-forward player-coach near Iowa, ~$90K+ floor, ~mid-2027. Deeper context: `~/DEV/ContextVault` — read before career/personal advice.\n\n## Preferences\n- Python + R. **macOS since 2026-08-05**; Windows 11 box still ACTIVE (synced 9/9). Tool paths: see CLAUDE.md.\n- Local SQLite/DuckDB; no cloud/servers/schedulers without Baylor IT approval. Storage = Google Drive only (OneDrive dropped 9/13); DuckDB stays local — cloud sync locks it.\n- Tangible working examples over documentation.\n\n## Working Style\n- Direct; says \"stop\" when done. Wants to be challenged, not affirmed — push on vague or rehearsed reasoning (\"sharpened, not soothed\").\n- Defers technical micro-decisions when convention exists — decide, state it, move on.\n- Builds incrementally. Nothing that breaks mid-season.\n- Authors own direction when leadership doesn't; OKR-driven change agent in a status-quo org.\n- Right question over downstream craft; Christian faith is his organizing frame.\n\n---\n\n## Working memory (global-memory/MEMORY.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 2,500 chars. Working scratchpad; writes take effect next session. -->\n# Working Memory\n\n## Active Threads\n- **Prioritization slate (9/14)**: LIVE. 5 active job projects w/ charters, cap 5 (Fall only), timer via ritual-check. Clear nags: `portfolio.py mark-done`. →Tier A prioritization-slate.\n- **Personal site (9/2)**: NEXT: phone check; host or keep local. →Tier A personal-site-build-routes.\n- **Parked 9/14**: Baseball profiles, Acro pass builder (waiting on coach), 6 more job projects. Kinexon VB DONE (Wenger, McGuyre use it).\n- **RTP CLA course (9/10)**: NEXT: import .ris → Zotero; lesson 0002. →Tier A rtp-cla-course.\n- **ATH Tech framework (8/14)**: shipped. Micah silent ~4wk; ITS pending. Inventory: only VB out; refresh 9/29. →Tier A ath-tech-framework, tech-inventory-refresh.\n- **Status report**: #2 send by 9/18, #3 by 9/25, then biweekly to 12/18 (timer checkpoints). →Tier A supervisor-log-pipeline.\n- **Open PRs**: AgentAssist #1 (draft), CFB_Analytics_Lab #1.\n- **GPS pull (9/13)**: LIVE Mac 06:30; Windows step OFF. NEXT: rotate token. →`DataBase_GPS_Reporting/docs/STATUS.md`\n- **Win refresh task (9/13)**: ForcePlate step broken, Perch untested. DECIDE which machine owns forceplate/perch DBs first. →`_INBOX/docs/STATUS.md`\n- **FB matchups (9/13)**: Wk3 keys + profile v2 on Drive. EPA = PFF current; CFBD PPA cross-check only. TRAP: `run_direction` != point of attack.\n\n## Environment Notes\n- Hooks: patch both `.claude/hooks/` and `.codex/hooks/`. →Tier A hook-two-copies-trap.\n- `~/DEV` = THIN repo; `.gitignore` starts `/*` — never remove. →Tier A github-consolidation.\n- Schedules →automation-cadence. CFB capture (Mon, 8/24–1/23) →cfb-forecast-capture. Portfolio dashboard →command-centre.\n\n## Pending Decisions\n- **OKR gate scored 9/14; still undecided**: AI integration buy-in + data governance (Anthony's proposal). Fold into NCAA charter or parking lot?\n- **Kitman vs MyLink**: MyLink (~half Kitman cost) + Kitman API access. Undecided; blocks priority 3.\n\n---\n\n## Cross-harness activity (KeystoneOS unified snapshot)\n\nNot loaded here — read it only if a question needs it.\nPath: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/unified-snapshot.md` (2642 chars, updated 2026-09-14 11:00)","<EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\nYou have superpowers.\n\n**Below is the full content of your 'superpowers:using-superpowers' skill - your introduction to using skills. For all other skills, use the 'Skill' tool:**\n\n---\nname: using-superpowers\ndescription: Use when starting any conversation - establishes how to find and use skills, requiring skill invocation before ANY response including clarifying questions\n---\n\n<SUBAGENT-STOP>\nIf you were dispatched as a subagent to execute a specific task, ignore this skill.\n</SUBAGENT-STOP>\n\n<EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\nIf you think there is even a 1% chance a skill might apply to what you are doing, you ABSOLUTELY MUST invoke the skill.\n\nIF A SKILL APPLIES TO YOUR TASK, YOU DO NOT HAVE A CHOICE. YOU MUST USE IT.\n\nThis is not negotiable. You cannot rationalize your way out of this.\n</EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\n\n## The Rule\n\n**Invoke relevant or requested skills BEFORE any response or action** — including clarifying questions, exploring the codebase, or checking files. If it turns out wrong for the situation, you don't have to use it.\n\n**Before entering plan mode:** if you haven't already brainstormed, invoke the brainstorming skill first.\n\nThen announce \"Using [skill] to [purpose]\" and follow the skill exactly. If it has a checklist, create a todo per item.\n\n## Skill Priority\n\nWhen multiple skills apply, process skills come first — they set the approach, then implementation skills (frontend-design, etc.) carry it out. Brainstorming and systematic-debugging are Superpowers' most common process skills, but the rule holds for any of them.\n\n- \"Let's build X\" → superpowers:brainstorming first, then implementation skills.\n- \"Fix this bug\" → superpowers:systematic-debugging first, then domain skills.\n\n## Red Flags\n\nThese thoughts mean STOP—you're rationalizing:\n\n| Thought | Reality |\n|---------|---------|\n| \"This is just a simple question\" | Questions are tasks. Check for skills. |\n| \"I need more context first\" | Skill check comes BEFORE clarifying questions. |\n| \"Let me explore the codebase first\" | Skills tell you HOW to explore. Check first. |\n| \"I can check git/files quickly\" | Files lack conversation context. Check for skills. |\n| \"Let me gather information first\" | Skills tell you HOW to gather information. |\n| \"This doesn't need a formal skill\" | If a skill exists, use it. |\n| \"I remember this skill\" | Skills evolve. Read current version. |\n| \"This doesn't count as a task\" | Action = task. Check for skills. |\n| \"The skill is overkill\" | Simple things become complex. Use it. |\n| \"I'll just do this one thing first\" | Check BEFORE doing anything. |\n| \"This feels productive\" | Undisciplined action wastes time. Skills prevent this. |\n| \"I know what that means\" | Knowing the concept ≠ using the skill. Invoke it. |\n\n## Platform Adaptation\n\nIf your harness appears here, read its reference file for special instructions:\n\n- Codex: `references/codex-tools.md`\n- Pi: `references/pi-tools.md`\n- Antigravity: `references/antigravity-tools.md`\n- Hermes Agent: `references/hermes-tools.md`\n\n## User Instructions\n\nUser instructions (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, GEMINI.md, etc, direct requests) take precedence over skills, which in turn override default behavior. Only skip skill workflows or instructions when your human partner has explicitly told you to.\n</EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>","## Today\nMonday, September 14, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n276 compiled articles (205 concepts, 70 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n# Daily Log: 2026-09-13\n\n## Sessions\n\n## Memory Maintenance\n\n### Memory Flush (02:37)\n\nFLUSH_OK - Nothing worth saving from this session\n\n### Memory Flush (21:44)\n\nFLUSH_OK - Nothing worth saving from this session\n","## Today\nMonday, September 14, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n276 compiled articles (205 concepts, 70 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n# Daily Log: 2026-09-13\n\n## Sessions\n\n## Memory Maintenance\n\n### Memory Flush (02:37)\n\nFLUSH_OK - Nothing worth saving from this session\n\n### Memory Flush (21:44)\n\nFLUSH_OK - Nothing worth saving from this session\n","\n<context_window_protection>\n  <priority_instructions>\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\n  </priority_instructions>\n\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \"timeline\")\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\n       - Each command: {label: \"section header\", command: \"shell command\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\"q1\", \"q2\", ...])\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\n\n  <when_not_to_use>\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\n  </when_not_to_use>\n\n  <file_writing_policy>\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\n  </file_writing_policy>\n\n  <output_constraints>\n    <artifact_policy>\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\n    </artifact_policy>\n  </output_constraints>\n  <session_continuity>\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\n  </session_continuity>\n\n  <ctx_commands>\n    \"ctx stats\" | \"ctx-stats\" | \"/ctx-stats\" | context savings question\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\n\n    \"ctx doctor\" | \"ctx-doctor\" | \"/ctx-doctor\" | diagnose context-mode\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx upgrade\" | \"ctx-upgrade\" | \"/ctx-upgrade\" | update context-mode\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx purge\" | \"ctx-purge\" | \"/ctx-purge\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\n\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\"\n  </ctx_commands>\n\n</context_window_protection>"],"hookName":"SessionStart","toolUseID":"SessionStart","hookEvent":"SessionStart"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"ab6c9a3f-941d-46f7-ab80-a03dcfc6d971","timestamp":"2026-09-15T03:05:25.360Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"9c1fe48d-8404-456e-b65e-61c070870312","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"ab6c9a3f-941d-46f7-ab80-a03dcfc6d971","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"493a214e-3257-4276-bea3-44fb06acbb14","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"Review the conversation context below and respond with a concise summary\nof important items that should be preserved in the daily log.\nDo NOT use any tools — just return plain text.\n\nFormat your response as a structured daily log entry with these sections:\n\n**Context:** [One line about what the user was working on]\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- [Important Q&A or discussions]\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- [Any decisions with rationale]\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- [Gotchas, patterns, or insights discovered]\n\n**Action Items:**\n- [Follow-ups or TODOs mentioned]\n\nSkip anything that is:\n- Routine tool calls or file reads\n- Content that's trivial or obvious\n- Trivial back-and-forth or clarification exchanges\n\nOnly include sections that have actual content. If nothing is worth saving,\nrespond with exactly: FLUSH_OK\n\n## Conversation Context\n\n**Assistant:** dcg blocked the shell redirect. Using the Write tool instead.\n\n**Assistant:** Now updating the index line so the hook matches the wider trap.\n\n**Assistant:** Commit landed; remote moved under me. Merging and retrying.\n\n**Assistant:** Saved — will be active from next session.\n\n**Done:**\n- `project_settings-json-bom-trap.md` rewritten. It now covers both faults: the BOM **and** the mangled characters, plus the fact that `CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.md` get hit too, not just `settings.json`.\n- Index line in `MEMORY.md` updated so the hook mentions the junk statusline bars.\n- Committed and pushed.\n\n**One thing you should look at**\n\nWhile pushing, a new commit came down from your Windows laptop:\n\n```\ncd8e9bb  RECOVERY-INVENTORY: the Windows laptop has no working backup\n```\n\nIt added `~/.claude/RECOVERY-INVENTORY.md` and 5 new PowerShell survey scripts. That is not something I did, and the title sounds important.\n\nWant me to read it and tell you what it says?\n\n**User:** yes, read it\n\n**Assistant:** Read it. Here is the short version.\n\n## What it says\n\n**Your Windows laptop has no working backup.** Anything pushed to GitHub is safe. Everything else on that machine exists in **one place only**.\n\n**Safe:** all 131 repos, `~/.claude`, skills, global memory, Google Drive documents.\n\n**Not safe — about 15 GB, single copy:**\n\n| Thing | Size |\n|---|---|\n| `SportScienceResearchRepo` working data | 4.37 GB |\n| `Football` folder | 2.94 GB |\n| `Risk_Stratification_Engine` | 1.80 GB |\n| `ForcePlate_DecisionSystem` (with the database) | 1.69 GB |\n| `~/.codex` session history | large |\n\n**Why it broke — three reasons:**\n\n1. All nine KeystoneOS scheduled tasks on Windows are **Disabled**. Switched off during the Mac move, never switched back on.\n2. Two replacement tasks were written, but **registered with no trigger**. They say `Ready`. They will never fire. No last run time, no next run time.\n3. The backup manifest on Windows is the **Mac's manifest**. I checked it — every path is a Mac path (`/Volumes/CRASHBOX`, `/Users/kristinarash/...`). Only the GitHub line works on Windows. Last backup state written there: **2026-08-14, status ERROR**. Nearly a month stale.\n\n## One thing in the report is wrong\n\nIt says the two backup scripts sit in `DEV\\_INBOX\\` and are \"not in any repo either\" because `dev-sync` skips folders starting with `_`.\n\n**That is not true.** I checked:\n- `dev-sync` only skips `.claude`, `_tools`, `_DRIVE`, `token-dashboard`, and `*_OLD_merged*`. `_INBOX` is not on that list.\n- `_INBOX` is its own repo at `github.com/erash11/_INBOX`, clean and pushed.\n- Both scripts are on this Mac right now at `~/DEV/_INBOX/backup_dev.ps1` and `backup_ai_sessions.ps1`.\n\nSo the scripts **are** backed up. Fix #2 in that report is unnecessary. The other findings still stand.\n\n## The real fix\n\nThe report ranks it right. Item 1 is almost all the value:\n\n**Give the two Windows tasks a trigger.** They are already written and registered. One `schtasks` change each. That is the whole difference between 15 GB single-copy and 15 GB backed up.\n\nOne warning worth keeping: `backup_dev.ps1` uses `/MIR`, which mirrors. If you delete something by accident, a mirror copies the deletion into your backup. Change it to `/E`.\n\n## Your call\n\nI cannot run `schtasks` from here — it must happen on the Windows laptop.\n\n- **A)** I fix the scripts now on the Mac (drop `/MIR`, add a Windows manifest), push them, and write you the exact `schtasks` commands to paste on Windows.\n- **B)** Leave it until you are next on Windows.\n\nI'd go with A. The edits are safe from here, and it means your Windows session is one paste instead of a debugging job.\n\n**User:** A\n\n**Assistant:** Both scripts read. `backup_ai_sessions.ps1` is already additive (`/E`) — good. Only `backup_dev.ps1` uses `/MIR`. Let me see the manifest structure.\n\n**Assistant:** There is already a `daily-backup.ps1` for Windows that reads the manifest. Let me check it before I add anything.\n\n**Assistant:** The picture changed. Let me think this through before editing.\n\n**Assistant:** Good — that confirms the plan and adds two traps I'd have missed. Applying now.\n\n**Assistant:** Now the Windows manifest overlay.\n\n**Assistant:** Both edits are correct. Now writing the paste-ready Windows commands, then committing.\n\n**Assistant:** Done. Let me verify what landed.\n\n**Assistant:** All done and pushed. Here is what you have.\n\n## What I changed\n\n**1. `~/DEV/_INBOX/backup_dev.ps1`** — changed `/MIR` to `/E`.\n\nNow it adds files and never deletes. Before, if you deleted something by accident, the next backup would copy that deletion into your backup too.\n\n**2. `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.windows.json`** — new file.\n\nRecords the real Windows backup destinations. Before, Windows was reading the Mac's list, where only the GitHub line works.\n\nBeing honest: **nothing reads this file yet.** It is documentation until someone builds a Windows checker. I wrote that inside the file so it does not look more finished than it is.\n\n**3. `~/DEV/_INBOX/FIX-WINDOWS-BACKUP.md`** — new file. This is the main thing.\n\nPaste-ready commands for the Windows laptop, in order.\n\n## What you do on Windows\n\nRun `/dev-sync` there first, then open `DEV\\_INBOX\\FIX-WINDOWS-BACKUP.md` and work down it.\n\nThree things in it you would have hit otherwise:\n\n- **`schtasks /Change` will not work.** It cannot add a schedule to a task that has none. The file uses `Set-ScheduledTask` instead.\n- **`G:` only exists when you are logged in.** It is a per-user Google Drive mount. If the task runs as SYSTEM, it sees no `G:` and writes nothing, silently. The file checks for this.\n- **A 2 AM trigger on a sleeping laptop never fires.** So each task gets two triggers: a set time *and* at-logon, plus `-StartWhenAvailable` so a missed slot catches up.\n\nThe file ends by making you check that **bytes actually landed in Google Drive** — not just that the task exists. That is the check the whole report says was missing everywhere.\n\n## One thing I found that the report got wrong\n\nI said earlier its fix #2 was unnecessary. Now I know why it matters that you skip it: the scheduled task was registered pointing at `DEV\\_INBOX\\`. **Moving the scripts would break the task.** Leave them where they are.\n\n## A warning I added\n\n**Do not re-enable the nine KeystoneOS tasks**, including `KeystoneOS-DailyBackup`.\n\n`~/.claude/scripts/daily-backup.ps1` is no longer the Windows script. It was replaced by the Mac version on 2026-08-05 — it uses `rsync` and Mac paths. Turning those tasks back on would run the Mac script on Windows and fail. The old Windows one is frozen at `daily-backup.ps1.win-orig`.\n\n## Heads up\n\nYour Windows laptop is pushing commits **right now**, while I was working. Three separate pushes landed and I had to merge each time:\n\n- `RECOVERY-INVENTORY` (22:30)\n- `survey-*` scripts\n- `verify-crossmachine.ps1`, `review-prep.ps1` (just now)\n\nSomething is actively running over there. If that is you, fine. If not, worth a look.\n\n**User:** <task-notification>\n<task-id>bpnwwmrvm</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01SeLaSFZrfeyg1pAAsaRTiQ</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/7741462f-dac7-4551-8692-76574bbfd202/tasks/bpnwwmrvm.output</output-file>\n<status>killed</status>\n<summary>Background command \"Find local folders for the five repos flagged missing\" was stopped because the system is running low on memory</summary>\n</task-notification>\n\n**Assistant:** That was the slow folder search I started earlier and replaced with a faster one. It got killed for using too much memory.\n\n**No action needed.** I already answered that question another way — all five repos were accounted for (`AI_Analyst` in `_ARCHIVE`, `Linda Projects`, `Website`, `claude-global-memory`, and `Injury_Surveillance` which I cloned).\n\nNothing is lost. Your sync and the Windows backup fix are both finished and pushed.\n\n**User:** do i need to do anything with this?\n\n**Assistant:** No. That one is closed — nothing for you to do.\n\nLet me check one thing that did change while we worked.\n\n**Assistant:** **No. Nothing to do with the killed task.** That is closed.\n\nBut something else landed on your Mac while we worked, and you should know about it:\n\n## Your skills got cut from ~160 to 63\n\nA commit called **\"apply global skill audit\"** came in at **12:12 today**, authored by you. It deleted **913 files**. It is already applied here and pushed. Your Mac and GitHub agree.\n\nIt also wrote a new memory note: `project_skill-bloat-and-promotion.md` — \"the 2026-09-12 skill audit (160→61), the project-until-promoted rule\".\n\nSo this looks **deliberate**, done by you from the other machine.\n\n## Two things to check\n\n**1. Was this you?** If you ran a skill audit today, you are fine — ignore the rest.\n\nIf you did **not** do this, tell me and I will look. Nothing is lost either way — `git` keeps every deleted skill, and I can bring any of them back.\n\n**2. `/backup` is still not run.** From this morning's list, and still true. Your CRASHBOX SSD backup has not happened.\n\n## Everything from this session is finished\n\n- DEV sync: clean, 136 repos, 1 cloned\n- Statusline: fixed\n- `CLAUDE.md` + `AGENTS.md`: encoding repaired\n- Memory note: updated\n- Windows backup fix: written and pushed to `DEV\\_INBOX\\FIX-WINDOWS-BACKUP.md`\n\nThe only open item you own is running that Windows file when you are next on that laptop.\n\n**User:** what windows file? what do I need to run?\n\n**Assistant:** My wording was bad. It is **not a file you run.** It is a page of instructions with commands to copy.\n\n## The problem it fixes\n\nYour Windows laptop has **no working backup**. About 15 GB there exists in one place only. If that laptop died today, it is gone.\n\nThe cause: two backup tasks exist on that laptop, but nobody gave them a **start time**. They sit there saying \"Ready\" forever and never run.\n\n## What you do\n\nNext time you are on the Windows laptop:\n\n**1.** Run `/dev-sync` there. That pulls down what I wrote today.\n\n**2.** Open this file and follow it:\n\n```\nC:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\_INBOX\\FIX-WINDOWS-BACKUP.md\n```\n\nIt is written as copy-paste blocks, in order.\n\n## The core of it\n\nIf you just want the main fix, this is it. Open **PowerShell 7 as admin**, logged in as you, and paste:\n\n```powershell\n$devTriggers = @(\n  (New-ScheduledTaskTrigger -Weekly -DaysOfWeek Sunday -At 6pm),\n  (New-ScheduledTaskTrigger -AtLogOn -User \"$env:USERDOMAIN\\$env:USERNAME\")\n)\nSet-ScheduledTask -TaskName \"Backup DEV to Drive\" `\n  -Trigger $devTriggers `\n  -Settings (New-ScheduledTaskSettingsSet -StartWhenAvailable -ExecutionTimeLimit (New-TimeSpan -Hours 4))\n\n$aiTriggers = @(\n  (New-ScheduledTaskTrigger -Daily -At 5:30pm),\n  (New-ScheduledTaskTrigger -AtLogOn -User \"$env:USERDOMAIN\\$env:USERNAME\")\n)\nSet-ScheduledTask -TaskName \"Backup AI Sessions to Drive\" `\n  -Trigger $aiTriggers `\n  -Settings (New-ScheduledTaskSettingsSet -StartWhenAvailable -ExecutionTimeLimit (New-TimeSpan -Hours 1))\n```\n\nThen check it worked:\n\n```powershell\nGet-ScheduledTask | Where-Object TaskName -like \"*Backup*\" |\n  Get-ScheduledTaskInfo | Select-Object TaskName, NextRunTime\n```\n\n**`NextRunTime` must show a date.** If it is blank, it still will not run.\n\n## Why the file has more than that\n\nThree things can still silently break it, and the file walks you through each:\n\n- The task might be running as SYSTEM, which cannot see your `G:` drive\n- The task names might be different from what I guessed\n- The task might exist and fire but copy nothing\n\nThe last section makes you check that **files actually appeared in Google Drive** — not just that the task looks healthy. That is the whole lesson from your own recovery report."},"uuid":"36ae8da2-7923-4e2d-8a9d-fa9aaa531ae5","timestamp":"2026-09-15T03:05:26.716Z","permissionMode":"auto","promptSource":"sdk","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"9c1fe48d-8404-456e-b65e-61c070870312","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
